### Describe the bug

When the app under test fails in `IHost.StartAsync` (for example `ValidateOnStart` throwing `OptionsValidationException`), `WebApplicationFactory<T>.CreateClient()` is supposed to rethrow that exception. Intermittently it throws `ObjectDisposedException: Cannot access a disposed object. Object name: 'IServiceProvider'` from `DeferredHostBuilder.DeferredHost.StartAsync` instead, and the real startup error is lost.

It's a race between two threads:

1. `HostFactoryResolver` runs the app's entry point on its own thread with `stopApplication: false`. `builder.Build()` hands the host to the factory, and the entry point carries on into `app.Run()`.
2. `StartAsync` throws, and the `finally` in `HostingAbstractionsHostExtensions.RunAsync` disposes the host. After that, the entry point exits and `DeferredHostBuilder.EntryPointCompleted(exception)` faults the start TCS with the real exception.
3. Meanwhile, on the test thread, `DeferredHost.StartAsync` calls `_host.Services.GetRequiredService<IHostApplicationLifetime>()` to register on `ApplicationStarted`. If step 2 has already disposed the host, this throws `ObjectDisposedException` before the TCS is ever awaited.

In our CI it hits about 1 in 16 runs on busy x64 Linux agents, and never on an arm64 Mac.

### Expected Behavior

`CreateClient()` consistently throws the exception the app failed to start with (`OptionsValidationException` here), as it already does when the app throws before `Run()`.

### Steps To Reproduce

Widening the window with an overridden `CreateHost` makes it fail every time:

```csharp
// Program.cs
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddOptions<MyOptions>().Validate(_ => false, "invalid").ValidateOnStart();
var app = builder.Build();
app.Run();

public class MyOptions { }
public partial class Program { }
```

```csharp
// Test
public class StartupFailureTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void CreateClient_ThrowsStartupException()
    {
        using var factory = new SlowStartFactory();
        Assert.Throws<OptionsValidationException>(() => factory.CreateClient());
        // Actual: ObjectDisposedException
    }

    private sealed class SlowStartFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>
    {
        protected override IHost CreateHost(IHostBuilder builder)
        {
            var host = builder.Build();
            Thread.Sleep(500); // let the app thread fail and dispose the host first
            host.Start();
            return host;
        }
    }
}
```

Without the override, the same test fails intermittently under CPU load.

### Exceptions (if any)

```
System.ObjectDisposedException: Cannot access a disposed object. Object name: 'IServiceProvider'.
   at ServiceProviderServiceExtensions.GetRequiredService[T](IServiceProvider provider)
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing.DeferredHostBuilder.DeferredHost.StartAsync(CancellationToken)
   at HostingAbstractionsHostExtensions.Start(IHost host)
   at WebApplicationFactory`1.CreateHost(IHostBuilder builder)
   at WebApplicationFactory`1.ConfigureHostBuilder(IHostBuilder hostBuilder)
   at WebApplicationFactory`1.StartServer()
   at WebApplicationFactory`1.CreateClient()
```

### .NET Version

10.0

### Anything else?

- ASP.NET Core version: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing 10.0.12. `DeferredHostBuilder` is unchanged on `main`.
- Related: #58442 reports the same `ObjectDisposedException` from other call sites (`HostingListener.CreateHost`, and the `IServer` lookup in `WebApplicationFactory.ConfigureHostBuilder`). The fix proposed here only covers the `DeferredHost.StartAsync` stack and doesn't address those.
- Related: #38335. A July 2023 comment there reports this exact failure (`ValidateOnStart` failing in `app.Run()`, `ObjectDisposedException` at `DeferredHostBuilder.cs` line 152), worked around with test retries. That issue is about the broader, by-design behaviour of `DeferredHostBuilder.Build()` running the entry point on its own thread; the fix proposed here doesn't change that.

Proposed fix: in `DeferredHost.StartAsync`, if resolving `IHostApplicationLifetime` throws `ObjectDisposedException`, skip the `ApplicationStarted` registration and still await the start TCS. The host is only disposed once the entry point is on its way out, and `HostFactoryResolver` calls `entrypointCompleted` in a `finally`, so the TCS always completes with the app's exception (or with success). PR with a deterministic regression test: #69685.

